  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "MonoBehaviour.OnRenderObject() gets called on every frame on every script in the scene that has it declared.\nUdonBehaviour having every Unity event declared causes OnRenderObject() to be called on every UdonBehaviour in the world even if they don't have anything in them, which can quickly add up to a significant increase in frametime.\nWith 10000 empty UdonBehaviours in the scene I ended up with the following results:\n\t1) Unity Editor: ≈4.2ms (≈240 FPS)\n\t2) VRChat: ≈14.7ms (≈70 FPS)\nHere are 2 more worlds in VRChat as a point of reference:\n\t1) Empty plane: ≈2.4ms (≈410 FPS)\n\t2) 1000 empty UdonBehaviours: ≈5.2ms (≈190 FPS)\nI have attached an image of the Unity Editor deep profiler timeline showing that OnRenderObject() gets called on the UdonBehaviours at the end of the frame\nAlso in case of any misunderstanding about the meaning of \"OnRenderObject\" I want to state that it has nothing to do with having a renderer component attached to the same object - \"OnRenderObject is called after camera has rendered the Scene\"\nUnity also clearly states the following in their documentation: \"Be aware that this function can have a performance impact because it runs on for every GameObject with a script that uses this callback.\"\nhttps://docs.unity3d.com/ScriptReference/MonoBehaviour.OnRenderObject.html",
